{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>No one shall take a millstone [as pledge].</b> The opponents say that this pericah was joined to 'and he shall gladden his wife,' because it alludes to the marital bed, for it is forbidden that he withhold from the marital bed. This is folly and emptiness. They brought as proof 'she grinds for another after my wife' — and I have already explained it. The truth is that it is as it sounds: it is forbidden for a person to take a millstone as pledge, and the word 'take' is missing, as in וַתֹּלֶד לְלֵוִי (Genesis 29:34) — and there are many like this, for their explanation is: she bore [a son] to Levi — the one who bore [is] the subject." "<b>Millstone.</b> A dual noun, for it is not possible for them to be separated." "<b>And the upper stone.</b> It is the female [stone] with water [?]. And one says: the wood that is upon the millstones. And one says: the lower [stone is] 'mill' and the upper [is] ' rider.'" "<b>For it is a life [that he pledges].</b> For by it one sustains lives. Those who rely on the adjacent placement of the pericah — it is no argument, for every commandment stands on its own, and adjacencies are in the manner of derash. This pericah is joined because he mentioned: that one must not take grapes to put into his vessel, and a sickle among the standing grain, and taking a wife and she became his and also another's, and she went out of his domain, and the one who takes a wife that he may rejoice in her — he shall not be wronged by going out to the army. And seizing the millstones is wronging, and also stealing a life. And likewise the plague of tzara'at, that they do not guard its laws — it harms those nearby. And likewise 'you shall not wrong the poor man in his pledge,' and the hired laborer, and young children — that they not die for their father's sin, and perverting the justice of the stranger and orphan, and taking a widow's garment as pledge, and the forgotten sheaf, for it belongs to the poor, and likewise 'you shall not maltreat,' and likewise 'lest he add and she be degraded,' and muzzling the ox — wronging, for he labors and does not eat. And the stubborn and rebellious son — in the likeness of wronging. And the seizing of the genitals — wronging. Diverse weights and diverse measures — actual wronging. And Amalek too wronged Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The phrase תטחן לאחר אשתי appears to cite Judges or a midrashic saying; the reference is uncertain and the hyper-literal rendering preserves the ambiguity. Ibn Ezra's grammatical point concerns an implied missing verb of taking/pledging, analogous to an implied subject in the Genesis verse.", 3 "ההוה במים is textually obscure; rendered hyper-literally. The sentence appears possibly corrupt, as 'the female [stone]' with 'water' does not correspond to any known identification of millstone parts. Also, the long catalogue of commandments from Deut 22–25 is rendered as a compressed run-on, preserving Ibn Ezra's terse style."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:24:52.613282825Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}